WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.340
Java can start external processes through APIs such as Runtime.exec and ProcessBuilder.

2
00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:08.600
That power should be treated carefully.

3
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:21.820
Command injection risk appears when user-controlled input influences an operating system command, argument, filename, path, flag, environment variable, working directory, or executable lookup.

4
00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:30.000
Even when the feature is legitimate, such as image conversion or report generation, string-built commands can blur the line between data and process instructions.

5
00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:33.960
The safest option is often not launching a process at all.

6
00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:38.540
Prefer Java libraries or platform APIs when they can perform the operation directly.

7
00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:43.840
If an external process is necessary, avoid building one large command string.

8
00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:52.820
Use APIs that pass the executable and arguments as separate values, choose the executable path deliberately, and map user choices to approved operations.

9
00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:59.480
A request should not be able to select arbitrary tools, flags, paths, or environment settings.

10
00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:02.480
File paths deserve special handling.

11
00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:08.420
A filename supplied by a user should become a controlled storage object, not a raw filesystem decision.

12
00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:19.400
Normalize and constrain paths to approved directories, generate server-side names where possible, and treat extensions and media types as policy decisions rather than trust signals.

13
00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:26.860
Process working directories and PATH behavior should be explicit so the application does not accidentally run a different executable than intended.

14
00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:28.900
Logging also needs care.

15
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:36.520
It is useful to record which controlled operation ran, whether validation failed, and which request or job triggered the action.

16
00:01:36.620 --> 00:01:44.940
It is usually not useful to store full raw command lines containing sensitive values, confusing characters, or user-supplied text.

17
00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:58.800
Secure process execution combines safe APIs, allowlisted operations, least privilege, controlled paths, bounded timeouts, and logs that support investigation without creating new exposure.
